      "evidence": ".booklet-work/reasoning-ratings/calls/enrichment-maths-1-part7-20261008/cc3bb7a393c0b10711a86b28dbe9580e389fbb4e5bbc8962c242ec1335c91774-1792558385968/last-message.txt"
    }
  },
  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Consider the following:\n$$\\begin{aligned}x_1&=1^2+2^2+2^2\\\\x_2&=2^2+3^2+6^2\\\\x_3&=3^2+4^2+12^2.\\end{aligned}$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p8-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Continuing this pattern, what is $x_4$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x_4=4^2+5^2+20^2=441$.",
          "worked": "The third squared number is the product of the first two numbers.\n$$\\begin{align*}x_4&=4^2+5^2+(4\\times5)^2\\\\x_4&=16+25+400\\\\x_4&=441.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "What is $x_n$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x_n=n^2+(n+1)^2+[n(n+1)]^2$.",
          "worked": "The first two squared numbers are consecutive integers; the third is their product.\n$$x_n=n^2+(n+1)^2+[n(n+1)]^2.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q14-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that $x_1$, $x_2$, $x_3$ and $x_4$ are perfect squares.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x_1=3^2$, $x_2=7^2$, $x_3=13^2$, $x_4=21^2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x_1&=1+4+4=9=3^2\\\\x_2&=4+9+36=49=7^2\\\\x_3&=9+16+144=169=13^2\\\\x_4&=16+25+400=441=21^2.\\end{align*}$$\nEach is the square of an integer.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q14-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Prove that $x_n$ is always a perfect square.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x_n=[n(n+1)+1]^2$.\n\nExpanding both expressions gives $n^4+2n^3+3n^2+2n+1$, so $x_n$ is the square of an integer.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x_n&=n^2+(n+1)^2+[n(n+1)]^2\\\\x_n&=n^2+n^2+2n+1+(n^2+n)^2\\\\x_n&=2n^2+2n+1+n^4+2n^3+n^2\\\\x_n&=n^4+2n^3+3n^2+2n+1.\\end{align*}$$\nAlso,\n$$\\begin{align*}[n(n+1)+1]^2&=(n^2+n+1)^2\\\\{}[n(n+1)+1]^2&=n^4+n^2+1+2n^3+2n^2+2n\\\\{}[n(n+1)+1]^2&=n^4+2n^3+3n^2+2n+1.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x_n=[n(n+1)+1]^2$. Since $n(n+1)+1$ is an integer, $x_n$ is always a perfect square.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      }
    ]
  },
